# oas-truth — how this repository is run

Reads OpenAPI (`.json` / `.yaml`) or TypeSpec (`.tsp`) as the single source of truth and returns
a typed `OpenAPI` document, plus the component code builders (headers, parameters, responses,
request bodies, callbacks, path items, examples, links, security schemes) that turn its
`components` into validator code for Zod, Valibot, ArkType, TypeBox or Effect Schema. Published
to npm as `oas-truth`. Code generators build on it, so a change to what it returns or emits
reaches every one of them. Built with vite-plus (`vp pack`, `vp check`, `vp test`), installed
with pnpm.

The config files are the source of truth: when an instruction contradicts one, quote the config
and ask before changing it.

Everything committed is written in **English** — code, comments, TSDoc, Markdown, commit
messages, workflow comments. textlint and cspell assume it. Test data may be in any language.

## Layout

```text
packages/oas-truth/           the published package (the only npm artifact)
  src/                        implementation; see the architecture rule for the layering
  src/index.ts                the public API: everything users can import is re-exported here
  vite.config.ts              pack (build), test and lint for the package — the lint comments
                              are the design
  tsconfig.json               NodeNext, strict, exactOptionalPropertyTypes; tests are excluded
vite.config.ts                shared formatting style (printWidth 100, single quotes, no semicolons)
lint/actionlint.mjs           runs actionlint (the npm WebAssembly build) over .github/workflows
.github/workflows/test.yaml   CI (Test): build, test, `vp run check`
.github/workflows/lint.yaml   CI (Lint): the repository linters, zizmor included
.github/workflows/release.yml after Test on main: tag, npm publish, GitHub release
```

## Commands

Run these from the repository root.

| Goal | Command |
| --- | --- |
| Install dependencies | `pnpm install --frozen-lockfile` |
| Build `dist` | `vp run oas-truth#build` |
| Unit tests | `pnpm test` |
| One test file | `pnpm -F oas-truth test src/<path>.test.ts` |
| Format check, lint and type check of the package, then the repository linters | `pnpm check` |
| Autofix formatting, oxlint, markdownlint and textlint | `pnpm fix` |
| Repository linters (markdownlint, textlint, cspell, secretlint, actionlint, zizmor) | `pnpm lint` |

- `pnpm check` does not run the tests; `pnpm test` does. CI runs both.
- zizmor is not an npm package. `pnpm lint` skips it with a note when it is not on `PATH`
  (`pipx install zizmor`); the Lint workflow installs it and fails without it.

## Definition of done

Before reporting a task finished, **`vp run oas-truth#build`, `pnpm test` and `pnpm check` must
all be green** — the Test workflow's steps, in its order. One failure means not done. If you only
ran part of it, say which part.

- A behavior change ships with a test that fails before it and passes after.
- A change to the public API or to what it returns updates `packages/oas-truth/README.md` in the
  same change (the root `README.md` is a symlink to it; see the docs rule).
- Report results as they are. Never call a task complete while hiding a failing command, and
  never make a check pass by weakening it.

## Invariants

- **Never relax a linter to get a change through.** Do not set a rule to `'off'` in `rules`, do
  not lower `categories`, do not drop `denyWarnings`, do not add an `ignorePatterns` entry to hide
  a finding. If a rule genuinely does not fit, say so and ask.
- **Suppressions live next to the code, naming the rule and the reason:**
  `// oxlint-disable-next-line rule-name -- reason`, or the reason on the line above. Never
  disable a whole file. `reportUnusedDisableDirectives: 'deny'` is on, so a suppression that
  stopped firing must go.
- **No `TODO` / `FIXME`.** `no-warning-comments` rejects them in source, textlint's `no-todo` in
  docs. Parked work belongs in the report.
- **`parseOpenAPI` never throws.** It returns `{ ok: true, value } | { ok: false, error }`, with a
  message the caller can show as-is. Keep every new failure path inside that result.
- **The code builders are pure**: data in, a string of source code out. No filesystem, no
  network, no global state.
- **Never hand-edit generated files:** `pnpm-lock.yaml` (let `pnpm install` write it), `dist/`,
  and the TypeSpec tests' `tmp-spec.tsp` / `tmp/` work files.
- **Never bump `version` in `packages/oas-truth/package.json`** unless the task is a release that
  was asked for: a new version reaching `main` is what publishes it. Never `npm publish` or push a
  `v*` tag by hand — `.github/workflows/release.yml` tags, publishes and releases, from one
  commit.

## Working order

1. Read the rules in force for the files you are about to touch (`lint.rules` and
   `lint.overrides` in `packages/oas-truth/vite.config.ts`, and the matching
   `.cursor/rules/*.mdc`).
2. Implement it the way the surrounding code is written. Do not introduce a second idiom.
3. `pnpm fix`, then the definition of done.
4. Commit and push (see Git policy).
5. In the final report state what changed, which commands verified it, and anything left out.

## Git policy

- Work on a branch and open a pull request to `main`; `main` is what releases. Never force-push
  and never rewrite pushed history.
- One logical change per commit. The subject is `type(scope): summary` in the imperative, no
  trailing period — `type` is one of `feat`, `fix`, `perf`, `refactor`, `docs`, `test`, `build`,
  `ci`, `chore`. The body explains why. A version bump is its own commit.
- Do not commit generated output, secrets or local tooling files.
